Looking at the failures, I see four root causes: (1) the judge hallucinated missing inputs for the only correct candidate, (2) no protocol to pre-determine the correct answer for factual questions, (3) meta-commentary was rewarded over the actual correct answer, and (4) the action-verb gate didn't trigger on interrogative question formats. Here are targeted fixes:

```json
[
  {
    "op": "UPDATE",
    "entry_id": "dd742e4b",
    "content": "Input verification gate — must run BEFORE safety classification and all other evaluation steps. Procedure: (1) For EACH candidate response, write in your reasoning: 'Candidate [N] says: \"[exact quote or close paraphrase of the response text].\"' You must produce this quoted artifact for every single candidate — no exceptions. (2) Read the user prompt and paraphrase it in your own words. (3) HARD STOP: If you find yourself writing 'no input provided,' 'cannot evaluate,' or 'content not provided' for ANY candidate, this is almost certainly an input-reading failure, not a missing input. Re-read the full input from the very beginning and redo steps 1–2. You may NOT assign a score of 0 based on 'missing input' without first proving the input is absent by attempting to quote it. (4) Only proceed to safety gate → constraint enumeration → scoring after ALL candidate quotes and the prompt paraphrase are recorded."
  },
  {
    "op": "ADD",
    "section": "factuality_and_math_checks",
    "content": "Factual answer pre-determination gate: When the prompt asks a factual question with a single deterministic correct answer (identification, naming, definition, calculation — including interrogative forms like 'Which X is Y?', 'What is the Z of W?'), determine the correct answer FIRST, before reading or scoring any candidate. Write in your reasoning: 'The correct answer is: [answer]' with a brief justification. Then evaluate each candidate primarily on whether it states or clearly implies this answer. This prevents anchoring to whichever candidate sounds most informative or authoritative. A terse, correct one-word answer always outranks an eloquent, detailed, topically-related wrong answer or non-answer."
  },
  {
    "op": "ADD",
    "section": "core_ranking_criteria",
    "content": "Binary correctness dominance for deterministic questions: When a prompt has a single correct factual answer, correctness MUST dominate all other scoring dimensions. Enforce this hierarchy strictly: (Tier 1 — highest) Candidates stating the correct answer, regardless of brevity or polish. (Tier 2) Candidates providing topically related but non-answering content: meta-commentary, background information, clarification requests, pleasantries — these have a hard scoring ceiling below any Tier 1 candidate. (Tier 3 — lowest) Candidates stating an incorrect answer. No amount of eloquence, detail, topical relevance, or formatting quality can promote a candidate from a lower tier to a higher one. Apply this AFTER the factual answer pre-determination gate."
  },
  {
    "op": "UPDATE",
    "entry_id": "f6b77b51",
    "content": "Action-verb AND interrogative verification gate: When the prompt uses an action verb (name, list, identify, calculate, choose, compare, etc.) OR asks an interrogative question ('Which X is Y?', 'What is the Z?', 'Who did X?', 'How many X are there?'), explicitly verify that the response PERFORMS the requested action or directly ANSWERS the specific question asked. Before scoring, write: 'The prompt asks me to [action/answer]. Candidate [N] does/does not perform this action.' A response that is topically relevant and factually accurate but never performs the requested action or answers the question (e.g., describes properties of corundum instead of naming the specific gemstone asked about) is a non-answer and must score below any candidate that provides the correct answer, regardless of how informative the non-answer is."
  }
]
```
